  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nThat pesky ridge continues to dominate the weather across Western\nNorth America. Hot and very dry conditions will continue through \nSunday. Highs in the 90s are expected each day with some locations\ntouching 100 on Saturday. Mid to upper level winds, due to this \nridge, will remain out of the WNW keeping the air through most of\nthe column very dry. RH values across the CWA will be in the \nteens to single digits every day through Sunday.\n\nDue to the primary flow of westerly winds, wildfire smoke from \nthe PNW will continue to be advected into our region. While the \nweak FROPAs this evening and Saturday will help push surface smoke\nto the south, the smoke aloft will linger for the foreseeable \nfuture. In addition to the wildfires in the PNW there are also a \nfew decent fires in west and SW Montana which will bring even more\nsmoke to locations in the western part of the CWA. Bozeman and \nLivingston were seeing reduced visibilities and unhealthy air \nquality through much of the afternoon and evening already on\nThursday. The cold frontal passages will have a hard time getting\nas far west as Livingston which may not allow for much of a break\nin the smoke. \n\nThe smoke concentrations will be almost the sole factor limiting\nhow hot temperatures get over the next few days. Clear skies and\nvery dry surface conditions are perfect for a rapid warm up under\nthis strong ridge, but smoke through the entire column will help \nscatter some of the sun's energy. Simply put, the more smoke in \nthe sky over the next few days, the harder it will be for \ntemperatures to warm up. Regardless, hot temperatures are near \ncertain through the weekend. \n\nWith all the hot and dry conditions, naturally there are fire\nweather concerns. Elevated fire weather conditions are forecast to\nlinger through the weekend due to the hot and dry conditions along\nwith breezy conditions where gusts between 10 and 20 mph are\nforecast. Saturday's winds will be slightly stronger due to a \ncold frontal passage. Widespread gusts over 20 mph are forecast \nwith localized gusts topping 30 mph. There are some disagreements \non just how strong the winds will be and a good portion of that \ncomes down to how high the mixing heights are able to reach. All \nmodels show them getting at least to the 700mb layer but some mix\nparts of the region up to 500mb. With how warm and dry the \nconditions will be, I am inclined to believe the more aggressive \nsolutions with mixing heights above 700mb, thus bringing down \nslightly stronger winds. \n\nAlthough the strength of the ridge into the weekend is stil a\nminor question, models are in decent agreement in the ridge \nbuilding over our area for the start of the work week. This will \nresult in continued slightly above average temperatures, hazy \nskies, and a potential return of monsoonal moisture and \nconvection. Although there is a lack of agreement how things shape\nup for the end of next week into mid August, there is a signal in\nmost models that there could be some sort of low pressure system \nthat breaks down the ridge and potentially gives us some active \nweather. WMR",
